With the new option `--decreases-orders` (off by default), a component of a `decreases` clause can name the well-founded order that compares it: `decreases e by O`, where `O` is a closed ghost expression of type `Std.Termination.Orders.Wfo<T>` and `e` has type `T`. `Wfo<T>` is a trait of a new standard-library module, loaded only under the option, whose members are a relation `gt` and a member `aha` that proves `gt` well-founded; `Of(r)` makes an order from a relation `r` proved well-founded. A call or loop iteration must make `O.gt(old e, e)` true, or leave `e` unchanged and decrease a later component. The option needs `--type-system-refresh` and `--general-traits=datatype` (or `full`). A callable with such a component must not be mutually recursive with `gt` or `aha`. Such components are not allowed in iterators, in `{:global}` or `{:callback}` clauses, or with `--legacy-decreases-order`. A program that does not use the option is unaffected. See https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/245.
